            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is generally in really good shape, however we did find 3 air leakage locations with the Infrared Camera (during the blower door air leakage test) that should be addressed:



** The attic access box (that the attic access panel fits into) is not solidly attached &amp; is hanging down, allowing a significant amount of air leakage (heat loss). I had you stand under it during the blower door test to feel the amount of airflow. On a winter day, that is the amount of heat escaping into your attic &amp; then outside. Warm, moist air entering the attic will immediately freeze during the cold winter months and can create a large amount of frost/ice inside the attic ........ damaging surrounding insulation. I could not see into the attic to check on this, as the attic access panel would not easily lift up, and I did not want to run the risk of forcing it open &amp; then not being able to get it back down in place again !!



** The wood trim above your master bedroom garden doors to the back is not well sealed along the top, and requires caulking



** The window in your sons bedroom on the main floor has a broken latch and will not seal. There was actually a lot of air leakage from this one window.



Savings: I would estimate that addressing the above 3 air leakage locations should reduce total air leakage heat loss by about 20%, that could potentially save $ 157 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 6.3 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

I have modeled a combination heating &amp; hot water system for this house - please see the recommendation in the Heating System section of this report.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

As relatively new owners of this house, this is your first winter heating season. From our discussion, it sounds like you have unfortunately had some troubles with the pellet boiler &amp; oil boiler that required 5 or 6 service call visits from a heating contractor.



The house was constructed in 2006, so the 2 existing heating systems are about 15 years old:



1. The oil boiler is a good brand and should last at least another 10 years - from the literature I found online, this Burnham LEDV-1 boiler has a 2006 date on it (so the original boiler) and had a rated Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE) of 86.7% ....... which is really good.



2. The pellet boiler (E-Compact 28) was installed into some new &amp; existing Yellowknife houses back 10+ years ago, and I'm unsure if it was installed in your house originally in 2006 or perhaps a few years later ? You have the unit that is manually loaded with pellets &amp; it looks like you purchase pellets in bags, by the pallet load. Our office uses an 80% annual efficiency for these wood pellet boilers. 



As discussed, in 2006 when your house was built, oil furnaces and boilers were the most common heating systems used - with AFUE's of 82% - 87%). Since the City of Yellowknife introduced a Building Bylaw in 2010 to require high energy efficiency of new housing, about 98% of all new houses have been installing propane furnaces &amp; boilers with AFUE's of 92% up to 96% - current standard is 95%.



I mentioned that I would model 2 options for your future planning:

1. Keeping the equipment you have (hoping you have no further issues) and model how much the annual savings would be if you could rely on using your pellet boiler consistently to offset the oil boiler.



2. Install a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing propane "combi boiler" (providing both heat &amp; domestic hot water) - eliminating the current oil &amp; wood pellet boilers.



Savings (based on the Arctic Energy Alliance fuel costs in Yellowknife @ December 1, 2020 - see Page 5)



1. Using the pellet boiler to offset the current oil boiler could is estimated to save $ 1,168 per year in energy costs, and increases your annual energy usage by 10.8 GJ (because of the lower comparable efficiency of the pellet boiler).



2. By replacing both the oil &amp; pellet boilers with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas "combi boiler" (providing both heat &amp; domestic hot water), you could potentially save $ 560 per year in energy costs, reduce your annual energy usage by 24.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, 3 bathroom fans, central hallway principal ventilation fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 10.4 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil boiler &amp; wood pellet boiler draw their combustion air from the outside of the house (ie: direct vent). If you replace the propane heater/stove in the lower level, please make sure you choose an appliance that is direct vent and draws it's combustion air directly from outside.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood fan

        - 3 x bathroom fans

        - 1 x Principal Ventilation Fan in the hallway on the top floor

        - The clothes dryer (I think the flexible vent pipe is ready to be replaced)



As mentioned during the evaluation:

1. The 2 top floor bathroom fans &amp; the principal ventilation fan all vent out through the soffit along the right rear of the house (3 x small round vent screens). From the ground level - these appear to have some dirt accumulating which may be restricting the performance of these exhaust fans.



2. The clothes dryer vent pipe where it goes through the wall appears to have a few small holes developing - it is likely due for a replacement.



For the lower level bathroom fan, you may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



